<p>One file, <code>depoth.yaml</code>, owns the whole shell: identity, directories,
navigation, redirects. It sits at the crate root.</p>
<h2 id="why-yaml-and-not-toml">Why YAML and not TOML</h2>
Why YAML and not TOML
<p>Two reasons, and both are practical.</p>
<p>The navigation tree is recursive. Groups nest inside groups, and real sites
nest five deep. TOML can express that, but only through repeated table-array
headers that are unreadable at that depth and give no visual cue about where
you are in the tree. YAML's indentation is the tree.</p>
<p>Frontmatter is already YAML. Using YAML for configuration too means a project
carries one parser and one set of quoting rules rather than two.</p>
