<h2 id="the-six-rules">The six rules</h2>
The six rules
<p>The rules are not invented. Each one is a bug that reached a human in an
earlier generation of this project, and every one of them was <strong>between</strong> pages
rather than inside one.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Rule</th>
<th>What it asserts</th>
<th>Why it exists</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>dead-link</code></td>
<td>Every internal <code>href</code> resolves to a file that was built.</td>
<td>A page was renamed and the links to it were not. The commonest failure by a distance.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>dead-asset</code></td>
<td>Every <code>img</code>, <code>script</code>, <code>link</code> and <code>source</code> target exists.</td>
<td>The same rename, applied to a stylesheet or an image instead of a page.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>duplicate-chrome</code></td>
<td>Selectors that must match exactly once, match exactly once.</td>
<td>A doubled navbar, and three stacked nav strips. A count that should be one and is two.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>incomplete-document</code></td>
<td>Each page has a doctype, a <code>lang</code>, a non-empty <code>&lt;title&gt;</code> and exactly one <code>h1</code>.</td>
<td>A fragment written to a route a browser can navigate to — which is what produced the doubled navbar upstream of it.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>links-into-a-partial</code></td>
<td>No <code>href</code> points at a fragment.</td>
<td>A fragment is fetched, never navigated to. A link to one hands the reader a page with no shell around it.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>headings</code></td>
<td>No heading level is skipped.</td>
<td>The outline is the document's structure for anything that cannot see the page.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><code>Policy</code> is what &quot;correct&quot; means for one site: which selectors must be unique,
which routes are allowed to be partial, and which link prefixes to leave alone.
The default requires exactly one <code>body &gt; header</code> and one <code>main</code> on every page.</p>
<p>External links, <code>mailto:</code>, <code>tel:</code>, <code>data:</code> and bare fragments are left alone.
Relative links resolve against the page's own directory.</p>
<p><code>Site::titles</code> groups pages by their <code>&lt;title&gt;</code>, which is how a template that
stamped one title onto everything shows up.</p>
<aside class="callout caution"><p class="callout__title">A check that cannot fail is not coverage</p><div class="callout__body"><p>The base-path rule shipped once with its <code>Policy</code> field wired up and the code
that used it missing, so it stored a value and did nothing. Every rule here has
