</div></aside>
<h2 id="why-it-runs-in-milliseconds">Why it runs in milliseconds</h2>
Why it runs in milliseconds
<p>There is no browser process, no HTTP server, no page load and no screenshot
comparison. The harness parses HTML in process and walks it with a CSS selector
engine. A browser suite pays a process launch and a real page load per
navigation, which is why it is measured in seconds, and it proves nothing about
these six rules that reading the files does not already prove.</p>
<p>The one dependency is <code>scraper</code> — html5ever's parser with a complete selector
engine, seventeen crates that a site's own binary never links. It is bought
deliberately: a harness that mis-parses HTML gives false confidence rather than
no confidence.</p>
<p>Each rule has a test with a fixture carrying exactly the bug it looks for. A
check that cannot fail is worse than no check, because it reads as coverage.</p>
<h2 id="what-is-left-to-playwright">What is left to Playwright</h2>
What is left to Playwright
<p>Three kinds of claim need a real browser, and they are run from the first page
rather than after the last:</p>
<ul>
<li>Layout at a breakpoint. Nothing about the DOM tells you two elements overlap
at 390 pixels.</li>
<li>A swap actually happening. The partial-navigation claims are asserted by
marking live elements and checking which marks survived the swap, which
requires a live DOM across a navigation.</li>
<li>A visual baseline, at several widths.</li>
</ul>
<p>The no-JavaScript pass also belongs there, because turning JavaScript off is a
browser setting. That every page works with JavaScript off is a test, not a
guideline.</p>

