<p>Paths are relative to the crate root. There is no <code>build.rs</code>: the macro
compiles the templates during expansion and emits an <code>include_bytes!</code> for every
file it reads, which is what makes cargo rebuild when a template changes.</p>
<h2 id="what-compiling-buys">What compiling buys</h2>
What compiling buys
<p>The template is type checked. A component called with the wrong argument, a
missing field, a renamed struct — all of them are compile errors naming the
line, not a blank region in a rendered page or a run-time panic on the one
route nobody opened.</p>
<p>Escaping is in the type system. <code>{expr}</code> renders any <code>Display</code> value with HTML
escaping applied, and nothing is allocated to do it: formatting goes into the
output buffer through an escaping adapter. <code>{! expr}</code> renders without escaping
and is the only way to emit HTML you built elsewhere, so an audit for XSS is a
grep for one token.</p>
<p>There is no template interpreter to maintain. An interpreter is a second,
weaker implementation of a compiler that already exists.</p>
<h2 id="what-compiling-costs">What compiling costs</h2>
What compiling costs
<p>A template edit pays for a compile. Measured on a thirteen-template probe with
a warm <code>target/</code>, that edit costs <strong>0.50 s</strong> — four times faster than the
Crystal predecessor's 2.0 s, because the heavy dependencies are compiled once
and only the leaf crate recompiles. A content edit pays nothing, because it
re-runs the binary you already have.</p>
<p>Two honest caveats. Codegen scales with template count, and 0.50 s is a
thirteen-template measurement. And a release build of the probe is 49 s, which
is a deploy cost rather than an authoring one — but it means <code>--release</code> must
never be the default for development.</p>
<p>The other cost is harder to price: a template is not editable without a Rust
toolchain. You cannot ship a theme as a folder of files that a reader edits in
place, and you cannot reload a template without a rebuild. depoth takes that
trade deliberately. The alternative is an interpreter, and the clock
measurements say the trade is not needed.</p>
<h2 id="layouts">Layouts</h2>
Layouts
<p>A layout is a component registered against a path prefix, and it wraps every
route beneath that prefix. Rendering folds from the innermost layout outward.
Each layout must mark the element that wraps exactly its children — see
<a href="/docs/partial-navigation">Partial navigation</a>, where that marker does its real
work.</p>

