<p>That marker is the whole trick. A partial contains every layout below the one
it starts at, because the server does not know where the visitor came from. The
client does — the old DOM is right there. dp-swap walks both trees down their
<code>dp-layout</code> markers, finds the last layer whose prefix matches on both sides,
and replaces only what is below it.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>navigation</th>
<th>deepest shared layer</th>
<th>what is replaced</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>/docs/guides/install</code> → <code>/docs/guides/deploy</code></td>
<td><code>/docs/guides</code></td>
<td>the guide body</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>/docs/reference</code> → <code>/docs/guides/install</code></td>
<td><code>/docs</code></td>
<td>the whole guides section</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>/about</code> → <code>/docs/guides/install</code></td>
<td>none</td>
<td>the entire outlet</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Put the marker on the element wrapping exactly the children and nothing else.
Anything a layout renders inside the marked element is replaced along with
them, so a sidebar inside the marker loses its scroll position on every
navigation — which looks like a working site that flickers rather than one that
breaks. <code>prerender check</code> fails the build when a layout forgets its marker or
drops its children. It cannot catch a marker in the wrong place.</p>
<h2 id="asking-for-less">Asking for less</h2>
Asking for less
<p>The client counts the layers it already holds and sends the number:</p>
<pre class="hl"><code class="language-text"><span class="hl-text hl-plain">GET /delta/card-6/activity      DP-Partial: 3
</span></code></pre>
<p>The server answers from <code>dist-partial/3</code>. Nothing is configured for this and
there is no manifest: the markers in the live document name the prefixes the
current page sits under, and the build writes one tree per depth up to the
deepest layout chain in the site. A server that does not understand the number
can treat any value as &quot;give me the fragment&quot; and serve depth 1.</p>
<p>All the forms live at the same URL. A request carrying <code>DP-Partial</code> is served
from a partial tree; everything else gets the document.</p>
<h2 id="chrome-that-is-not-swapped">Chrome that is not swapped</h2>
Chrome that is not swapped
<p>The deeper the swap, the more of the page keeps its old state — including the
sidebar link still highlighted for the section you left. A layout can be
registered with a second renderer whose output is applied out of band. It emits
elements carrying <code>dp-swap-oob</code> and an <code>id</code>, and the client applies them by
<code>id</code> before the main swap. Only the deepest skipped layout contributes
out-of-band markup to a given partial, so a response never carries replacements
for chrome the client is about to discard.</p>
<h2 id="--no-partials">--no-partials</h2>
--no-partials
<p>Partial navigation needs a server that can route on a request header. Some
hosts have none — GitHub Pages among them — and there the partial trees are
bytes nothing can ever ask for.</p>
<pre class="hl"><code class="language-sh"><span class="hl-source hl-shell hl-bash"><span class="hl-meta hl-function-call hl-shell"><span class="hl-variable hl-function hl-shell">cargo</span></span><span class="hl-meta hl-function-call hl-arguments hl-shell"> run<span class="hl-keyword hl-operator hl-end-of-options hl-shell"> --</span></span><span class="hl-meta hl-function-call hl-arguments hl-shell"> build --no-partials</span>
</span></code></pre>
<p>One tree, an ordinary static site. Navigation degrades to full page loads and
nothing else changes, because every page was already a complete document —
which is the same property that makes the site work with JavaScript off.</p>
<p>The switch is not a second rendering path. A test asserts the documents are
byte-identical either way, so the site you publish is the site that was tested.</p>

